A||N||7||Dan86gt3.8||I need help from anyone thats ever installed a rear swaybar on a pre 88 fiero!||||5||
Z||000000||Dan86gt3.8||01-15-2000||03:46 PM||||Yes, I was itrying to install my rear swaybar, and I had lost the instructions that came with it.  I rember them saying something about drilling the holes in the a-arm.  I also am having trouble trying to figure out which holes to use?  the ones up front look like it would keep the endlionk out of the way but the bar doesen't match up to thoose.  It matches up to the ones closer to the rear of the car, but the e-brake cable is in the way and the cv boot looks like it might rub!  Does anyone know which holes to use or know of instructions online or have a site with pics??????  Any help would be greatly appreciated!<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||daved||01-15-2000||06:52 PM||||Not to push other sites....but I find some very useful stuff on the "Fiero Online Service Guide"<BR>Try 'modifications' section and scroll down to 'rear sway bar addition'<BR>Let us know how you make out, <BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/mod.rear-sway-bar.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/MotorCity/Garage/5007/fiero_osg/mod.rear-sway-bar.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||sluppy123||01-15-2000||10:25 PM||||who did you get the sway bar from?(J.C. Whitney, Fiero Store...)I can scan my instructions from my bar (It's a j.c. whitney)<P>I think i used the hole's toward the front of the A arm. Did you use ramps or jack stands? The bar needs to be installed with the weight on the wheels, in it's normal ride height. Then the front holes should line up<BR>-Brian<p>[This message has been edited by sluppy123 (edited 01-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||01-15-2000||10:44 PM||||I installed the Addco bar from JC Whitney on my 85SE v-6 4spd and love it.<P>Use the front set of holes in the A-arm.  You'll have to drill them out to 1/2" diameter for the shoulder of the bushing to seat.  The sway bar will be mounted to the front side of the cradle with the end link sides facing to the rear.  Hook up the bar to the A-arms and tie the bar off to the frame to hold it up.  My sway bar has a bend in it to clear the exhaust pipe going between the catalytic converter and the mufflers.  With the weight of the car on the A-arms mark where the mounting brackets will mount to the cradle.  Drill out two holes for each side to mount the cradle brackets.  You can access the bolts through the holes in the side of the cradle where the brackets attach.  I don't remember the size of the holes for the bracket bolts.<P>On mine the sway bar is a little bit wider at the end links than the holes in the A-arms (maybe a bit less than an inch).  I had to coax them into the holes.<P>The E-brake cable will be a tight fit between the sway bar end link and the lower ball joint but it will fit.<P>Like I said, mine made quite a bit of difference in cornering.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
Z||000004||2birds||01-15-2000||10:46 PM||||I got the JC Whitney bar(7/8"), and ditto Slup's comments on the putting the weight of the car on the wheels.  It's a lot easier on a lift.  The brackets mount right by the hole in the subframe, by the forward control arm bushing if I remember right, so you can get to the nuts.  I also got a bigger end link bolt (3/8', I think).  The one that came with the bar was too thin, IMHO.  I got poly bushings for it too.  You'll be happy you got it. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Carrolles||01-24-2000||08:13 AM||||I know this is late in coming but I just found this excellent page on installing an Addco bar.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.tir.com/~jaski/swaybar.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tir.com/~jaski/swaybar.htm</A> <P>||||reg||1
